      DESIGN (binding for implementers; authority reviews this hardest)

      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D1 — Mechanism choice (spec left this open; plan decides)
      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Chosen: a defineGraceCommand wrapper around citty's defineCommand, plus a pure
      refuse helper it always calls. Host under M-QUERY in a **new** file
      src/query/command.ts with its own honest MODULE_CONTRACT (see D2). Co-located
      test: src/query/command.test.ts. src/query/errors.ts is **not** edited.

      Why not (1) a shared helper called manually at the top of each run(context):
      every future boolean-hosting leaf must remember the call. AC-CLASS-COVERAGE's
      failure condition — "a new type:boolean under src/ can ship without the loud
      refusal" — becomes a discipline failure, not a red test, unless every call site
      is also listed. A wrapper makes the definition site the only discipline, and a
      brand on the wrapped command object makes missing the wrapper detectable.

      Why not (3) a single entry-point guard before citty dispatches in grace.ts:
      src/grace.ts declares LINKS M-CLI, and M-CLI is **not** in the approved
      AffectedAreas. Editing grace.ts to install a root guard, export main, or change
      dispatch contradicts the closed list and is a stop-and-report. The in-scope path
      is the exported command objects (see D3) — no grace.ts edit.

      Why defineGraceCommand rather than only pure helper:
        - At run time re-read def.args for every arg whose type is "boolean" (same
          object the command declares — a new boolean key is covered without re-wrap).
        - Prepend refuseBooleanSpaceForm(context.rawArgs, names) before the original run.
        - Recursively wrap subCommands so factories like gateSubCommand("approve") that
          return defineGraceCommand({...}) protect each leaf without three copy-pastes.
        - Stamp a well-known brand (exported constant or Symbol.for(
          "ngrace.booleanSpaceGuard")) set true on the command def so the coverage test
          can detect "has boolean args but not wrapped".

      Pure helper responsibilities (export for unit test and for the wrapper):
        collectBooleanFlagNames(argsDef) → string[] of arg keys with type === "boolean"
        flagArgvTokens(name) → long forms an operator may type: --name and
          --kebab-case(name) when distinct (citty aliases camel/kebab for every arg —
          measured in citty parseArgs). No short aliases on booleans at plan time
          (re-check at execute; if an alias appears, include it for that flag only).
        refuseBooleanSpaceForm(rawArgs, flagNames):
          Scan rawArgs left-to-right. When token i is a long form of a declared boolean
          flag **without** `=` (e.g. `--record`, not `--record=false`), and token i+1
          is exactly the case-sensitive ASCII string `true` or `false`, throw
          GraceCommandError("invalid-arguments", message) — import GraceCommandError
          from ./errors; reuse existing code; no new lint/gate catalog entry.
          Message **must** name both working forms for that flag: equals
          (`--flag=true` / `--flag=false`) and the citty negation / bare forms
          (`--no-flag` and bare `--flag` means true). Do not guess intent and proceed.
          Do not rewrite argv and continue.
        Not an error: `--flag=true`, `--flag=false`, bare `--flag`, `--no-flag`, or a
        bare true/false whose previous token is not a boolean flag form (positionals,
        subcommands, string values).

      citty fact the guard depends on (measured, not assumed): _isValueFlag treats only
      string/enum as consuming the next token; boolean flags do not, so space-form
      true/false becomes a positional. That is F18 / F18.1. The guard inspects rawArgs
      before side effects; it does not re-parse with a forked citty.

      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D2 — Host file: new src/query/command.ts (D12; not errors.ts)
      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      **Do not put the guard in src/query/errors.ts.** That file declares:

        PURPOSE: Artifact query and navigation CLI
        SCOPE: Module, file, graph, and verification resolution

      defineGraceCommand wraps **every** CLI command (gate, cursor, status, lint,
      context, …). None of those are module/file/graph/verification resolution.
      Hosting there falsifies the declared SCOPE — a module contract that does not
      describe what the module holds is the defect class this track exists to remove.

      This is **D12 again, one file over.** D12 rejected src/artifact/paths.ts as host
      for listLooseEvents / listRunOrphans on exactly this ground (paths.ts SCOPE is
      root tags / companions / ARTIFACT_DIR; membership is XML-reading inventory) and
      put the definition in its own file with a contract true from birth
      (decisions.md D12; C-REPORT-HONESTY plan D1). Same shape here.

      **Authorized lint window (D12).** Adding a not-yet-created path to
      ObservedWriteScope raises change.graph-anchors-miss-write-scope
      (lint/core.ts:496–530 / isModuleOwnableWritePath) because linksByPath is built
      only from files that exist. D12 authorized that window: between plan authoring
      and T-001 creating the file, ngrace lint reports **exactly one** such error on
      the new production path. evaluateApproveGate (gates/core.ts:196–236) does **not**
      evaluate lint, so approval is unaffected. AC-SUITE-AND-LINT requires 0/0 at
      close (after T-001 creates the file with a true MODULE_CONTRACT), not throughout.
      An expected error the plan named in advance is not a surprise (P0.9's distinction).
      Optimizing around this window by falsifying errors.ts SCOPE is rejected.

      **New file MODULE_CONTRACT (verbatim shape for T-001; adjust only if execute
      discovers a truer DEPENDS line):**

        // START_MODULE_CONTRACT
        //   PURPOSE: Citty CLI argument-form guard
        //   SCOPE: Boolean space-form refusal and defineGraceCommand wrapper
        //   DEPENDS: none
        //   LINKS: M-QUERY
        //   ROLE: RUNTIME
        //   MAP_MODE: EXPORTS
        // END_MODULE_CONTRACT

      Imports: GraceCommandError from ./errors; defineCommand from citty. Does **not**
      import any CLI command module (leaf relative to command trees). Command modules
      import defineGraceCommand from ./query/command or ../query/command.

      Co-located src/query/command.test.ts (test paths are not module-ownable for
      graph-anchors; no MODULE_CONTRACT required). Do **not** add exports to
      errors.ts; its MAP_MODE EXPORTS map and SCOPE stay unchanged.

      **M-QUERY is the best host the approved AffectedAreas allows, not a natural
      one.** M-QUERY's graph summary is "Artifact query and navigation: module, file,
      graph, and verification resolution", yet it already hosts runGraceCommand and
      GraceCommandError (thirteen files import them CLI-wide). The module has already
      accreted cross-cutting CLI infrastructure beyond its stated scope; this guard
      adds to that accretion. The honest home would be a CLI-infrastructure module;
      creating one needs a new graph anchor the approved AffectedAreas does not grant.
      Recorded as a finding for the authority — not repaired in this bundle. Stop-and-
      report only if execute concludes M-QUERY cannot host even with an honest file-
      local contract (it can: LINKS M-QUERY on command.ts is allowed; the graph
      summary drift is module-level, not a blocker for a new co-located file).

      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D3 — Coverage from live command objects (not source text — anti-F10)
      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Denominator is derived by walking the **exported command objects**, not by
      grepping `type: "boolean"` in source (F10 shape: a regex standing in for a
      property). Import and walk:

        gateCommand, cursorCommand, statusCommand, lintCommand,
        moduleCommand, fileCommand, graphCommand, contextCommand,
        verificationCommand, reviewCommand, doctorCommand

      For each node: for each entry in cmd.args where def.type === "boolean", record
      { path, name, default }. Recurse cmd.subCommands. citty's defineCommand is
      identity (returns the def) — args and subCommands are live on the object
      (probed at plan authoring: gate approve.args.record.type === "boolean").

      **24 vs 22 (two numbers, two measures — neither is a typo):**

        Live-object walk at plan authoring HEAD 4bf483c (full list, not head):
          **24 boolean flag sites** on the composed command tree.
          gate approve/apply/archive each carry --record (3) because gateSubCommand
          is a factory invoked three times; plus 21 other live sites on cursor,
          status, lint, module, file, graph, context, verification.

        Source-text count `rg -n 'type: "boolean"' src/`:
          **22 declarations** in nine files. gateSubCommand contains **one** source
          declaration of record reused for three subcommands — a grep cannot see the
          factory expansion. The two numbers measure different things: source
          declarations vs live command-object sites. Class coverage is over **live
          sites** (what runs), which is 24.

      Re-measure at execute with the same walker; pin the exact count with toBe
      (F23 — never toBeGreaterThanOrEqual). If the tree moved, update the expected
      count from the walk output, not from memory.

      Brand check (structural half of AC-CLASS-COVERAGE): every command node that
      declares ≥1 boolean arg must carry the defineGraceCommand brand. A node with
      booleans and no brand fails the test. After T-002 only gate leaves are branded;
      full brand-all is T-003. The walker count (24) is available from T-001; the
      brand assertion for all 24 is T-003.

      Behavioral half: pure refuseBooleanSpaceForm tests for every flag name collected
      by the walker (synthetic rawArgs `--name false` / `--name true` each throw;
      `--name=false` does not). Plus CLI integrations for the incident shapes (D4)
      split across T-002 (gate --record) and T-003 (class + F18.1 positionals).

      Discrimination protocol (T-003, required once per bundle, record in attempt notes):
        Temporarily add a type:"boolean" flag to a composed command **using citty
        defineCommand directly** (or strip the brand / skip the wrapper) so the flag
        is outside the guard; run the coverage brand test; confirm it goes red;
        revert; confirm git status --short is clean of that probe. A coverage test
        nobody has ever seen fail is a claim, not a check.

      Honest coverage gap (named trade, not silent): the composed roots list is at the
      **command** level. A brand-new top-level command added only in grace.ts and not
      to the test roots array would escape the walk. A new **flag** on any existing
      exported command is caught. Closing the top-level gap would require editing
      grace.ts (M-CLI) — **out of AffectedAreas**. This plan accepts the command-level
      denominator and names the gap.

      reviewCommand and doctorCommand are in the walk (zero booleans today). They stay
      unedited unless a boolean appears; the walk still includes them so a future
      boolean on either without the brand is red.

      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D4 — F18 incident and F18.1 positional corruption
      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      F18: `ngrace gate <approve|apply|archive> --change C-* --record false` parses
